 - **Siege production — conditional minimization (trebuchets especially)**: In `CvCityAI::AI_chooseUnit`, added targeted checks to keep **siege output** as low as needed—**up to none at all** when conditions say they’re poor value (e.g., when the AI is **weaker** or not preparing **city assaults**). Rationale: **trebuchets** are very **hammer-inefficient** outside direct city attacks; **catapults/cannons** also aren’t versatile enough (and are often misused) for defense. Current tuning intentionally leans **low** versus before (but not permanently zero when other conditions are met). Result: **more varied offensive stacks** and **stronger defense** when behind. See [KI#53.3](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs_And_Appendixes/README_Known_Issues_In_Base_AdvCiv_Civ4.md#533-tremendously-improved-ai-overbuilding-siege-units-in-particular-trebuchets-but-not-only-where-they-are-detrimental-to-do-build-in-particular-when-weaker-but-not-only-anyways-etc). **Update:** Thresholds have been **slightly relaxed pre-Renaissance**; **extra-relaxed when holding key strategic bonuses** (**Iron**, **Copper**, **Horse**, **Camel**, **Elephants**) to enable offense windows (e.g., longbow + siege); and **heavily relaxed from Renaissance onward** so **Cannons** can serve as a defensive pivot and the AI avoids over-producing Pikemen into Cuirassiers, and more generally since later sieges units are stronger and more effective, to also have a more varied and effective military composition.
 - **City-counter role pruning (e.g. Macemen)**: Removed `UNITAI_CITY_COUNTER` from **non-versatile** units (e.g., `UNIT_MACEMAN`). For example for macemen, as city defenders they’re too easy to punish—knights counter them hard, and even crossbow stacks can beat defending macemen—so they’re better assigned to **offensive** `UnitAI` roles. While macemen beat pikemen, pikes seldom assault cities efficiently, so the city-counter slot isn’t warranted. The same adjustment applies to any other units with this mismatch.
 - **Mounted “Counter” role pruning:** Removed `UNITAI_COUNTER` from the mounted units (`UNIT_CHARIOT`, `UNIT_EGYPTIAN_WAR_CHARIOT`, `UNIT_CARTHAGE_NUMIDIAN_CAVALRY`) that had it. Rationale: mounted units don’t "receive defense bonuses" (note: not related to bonuses/ressources) and can be countered by other units; assigning them to COUNTER can park them in cities (e.g. seemingly seen in autoplay with War Chariots) and occupies a slot **Longbowmen** are more fit to fill as they unlock early and are cheap and versatile defenders, so these mounted unit lines are better used for offense roles rather during its window—or skipped outside it.
-- **Very cheap-combat spam cap (tiered & generalized):** The AI was **overbuilding very cheap combat units**—notably the **Ancient Maceman**—even ~T100. Because they’re so cheap, they pile up, are weak once Archers appear, and can drag the economy. We added conditions in `CvCityAI::AI_chooseUnit` to **limit** these builds, with a **stricter cap after ~T50** when they’re especially poor value. In AdvCiv-SAS, Archers arrive early, are cheap/versatile, and fill defense quotas sooner, letting cities pivot to offense. (Scouts get trimmed incidentally since they’re combat-tagged, though they weren’t the main issue.) **Result:** more varied, stronger stacks (e.g., ~3 Ancient Macemen @T100–130 vs ~11 before) and a **healthier economy**. See [KI#53.4](/_1_AdvCiv-SAS/Docs_And_Appendixes/README_Known_Issues_In_Base_AdvCiv_Civ4.md#534-tremendously-improved-ai-overbuilding-very-cheap-combat-units-ancient-macemen-only-being-an-issue-as-of-now-anyways-etc-in-the-early-game-sometimes-even-at-turn-100-which-is-inefficient-and-easy-to-overshoot-if-i-may-say-but-anyways-etc-as-they-are-cheap-and-accumulate-quickly-but-are-not-too-effective-especially-as-soon-as-we-get-archer-units-anyways-etc-now-limited-especially-even-more-so-after-as-of-now-turn-50-where-they-should-be-especially-useless-much-better-military-compositions-and-better-growth-as-a-result-anyways-etc). **Update:** The cap is now **generalized beyond Ancient** using **tier-based (not era-based) thresholds** to avoid era bleed-through (e.g., Ancient Macemen don’t inherit looser Classical caps). This also aims to trim (at least in theory, data lacking to draw a conclusion on an experiment anyways etc) for example **Archers in Classical** and **Longbows/Triremes in Medieval** when they’re too cheap for their tier. In practice we see AI mixes improve (e.g., Japan AI shifting from ~75 Longbows @T200 to ~50–60 Longbows + 10–15 Camel Archers + 20–27 Samurai, run-dependent). Tunables live in `CvCityAI::AI_chooseUnit` and [`GlobalDefines_advciv_sas.xml`](/Assets/XML/GlobalDefines_advciv_sas.xml). **Update (fallback production):** Applied the same cap inside `CvCity::doTurn`’s **no-production fallback** so that the **new offense-only** alternative logic no longer spams **Ancient Macemen** (e.g., ~19 @T100). (Requires AdvCiv-SAS 5115+) **Update 2 (Renaissance+ anti-pike / cheap melee):** Added extra gating for **Renaissance+ very-cheap land, direct-combat units** (no scouts, spies, naval units, etc.). Goal: reduce ineffective late-game spam of obsolete counters (e.g. pikes into guns) and improve economic efficiency (lower unit costs, with more hammers available for stronger units and/or additional buildings instead of obsolete high-maintenance units).
